\title{The Topological Kernel of Social Choice: What Borsuk--Ulam Does and Does
Not Say About Arrow's Theorem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A recurring slogan in mathematical folklore asserts that Arrow's impossibility
theorem is a corollary of the Borsuk--Ulam theorem, and that consequently every
continuous social choice function is either discontinuous or dictatorial. We
examine this claim rigorously and in two directions. First, we isolate the
genuine topological kernel of the picture: the one-dimensional Borsuk--Ulam
theorem, which states that a continuous $2\pi$-periodic function
$f:\R\to\R$ admits an antipodal coincidence $f(x)=f(x+\pi)$, and whose
social-choice reading is that no continuous, periodic scoring rule can strictly
prefer every opinion to its antipode. Second, we give an explicit disproof of the
over-strong conjecture: for every $n\ge 2$ the arithmetic mean is a continuous,
unanimous (Pareto), anonymous, monotone, translation-invariant, and
non-dictatorial aggregation rule on the contractible domain $\R^n$. This refutes
``continuous $\Rightarrow$ dictatorial.'' We conclude by identifying the correct
topological statements---Chichilnisky's impossibility theorem for aggregation on
spheres and Baryshnikov's topological derivation of Arrow's discrete theorem---and
explain precisely why the domain's topology, rather than the act of voting, is the
seat of the obstruction.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Two impossibility theorems anchor two different disciplines. In economics,
Arrow's theorem (1951) states that when there are at least three alternatives, no
rule aggregating individual rankings into a social ranking can simultaneously
satisfy unanimity (Pareto), independence of irrelevant alternatives (IIA), and
non-dictatorship. In topology, the Borsuk--Ulam theorem states that any
continuous map $f:S^n\to\R^n$ identifies a pair of antipodes, $f(x)=f(-x)$.

The conjecture under examination proposes a bridge: encode preference profiles
over $n$ alternatives as points on a ``preference sphere'' $S^{n-1}$, with
antipodal points representing reversed preferences; let $f:S^{n-1}\to\R^{n-1}$
report the social preference; and invoke Borsuk--Ulam to force $f(x)=f(-x)$, a
coincidence that supposedly contradicts Pareto efficiency. The proposed upshot is
a slogan---\emph{any social choice function on $n$ alternatives is either
discontinuous or dictatorial}---and a grand thesis: \emph{social choice is
topology; Arrow's impossibility is a theorem about spheres.}

Our contribution is to separate the true from the false with complete precision.
In Part~I (Section~\ref{sec:kernel}) we prove the one-dimensional Borsuk--Ulam
theorem and derive its faithful social-choice reading. In Part~II
(Section~\ref{sec:disproof}) we disprove the strong conjecture by exhibiting an
explicit continuous, unanimous, anonymous, monotone, translation-invariant,
non-dictatorial aggregator---the mean---on a contractible domain.
Section~\ref{sec:discussion} situates the honest thesis relative to
Chichilnisky's and Baryshnikov's theorems. The overarching finding is a clean
dichotomy: the Borsuk--Ulam obstruction requires a non-contractible domain (a
sphere); on a contractible domain the obstruction vanishes and fair continuous
aggregation exists.

\section{Preliminaries and definitions}

Throughout, ``continuous'' refers to the usual Euclidean topology.

\begin{definition}[Periodic function on the circle]
A function $f:\R\to\R$ is \emph{$2\pi$-periodic} if $f(x+2\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x$.
Such an $f$ is precisely a function on the circle $S^1=\R/2\pi\mathbb{Z}$; if in
addition $f$ is continuous it is a continuous map $S^1\to\R^1$. The \emph{antipode}
of the point $x\in S^1$ is $x+\pi$ (a half-turn).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Aggregation rule]
Fix $n\ge 1$ agents. Model each agent's opinion as a real number (a position on a
one-dimensional spectrum). An \emph{aggregation rule} is a map $F:\R^n\to\R$,
written $F(p_1,\dots,p_n)$ where $p_i$ is agent $i$'s position.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Axioms for an aggregation rule]
An aggregation rule $F$ is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{continuous} if it is continuous as a map $\R^n\to\R$;
  \item \emph{unanimous (Pareto)} if $F(c,\dots,c)=c$ for every constant $c$;
  \item \emph{anonymous} if $F(p_{\sigma(1)},\dots,p_{\sigma(n)})=F(p_1,\dots,p_n)$
    for every permutation $\sigma$;
  \item \emph{monotone} if $p_i\le q_i$ for all $i$ implies $F(p)\le F(q)$;
  \item \emph{translation-invariant} if $F(p_1+c,\dots,p_n+c)=F(p)+c$ for every
    constant $c$;
  \item \emph{dictatorial} if there is an agent $i$ with $F(p)=p_i$ for all $p$;
    \emph{non-dictatorial} otherwise.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Arithmetic mean rule]
The \emph{mean aggregator} is
\[
  \avg_n(p) \;=\; \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} p_i.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{Part I: the one-dimensional Borsuk--Ulam theorem}
\label{sec:kernel}

\begin{theorem}[One-dimensional Borsuk--Ulam]
\label{thm:bu}
Let $f:\R\to\R$ be continuous and $2\pi$-periodic. Then there exists $x$ with
$f(x)=f(x+\pi)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define the antipodal difference $g(x)=f(x)-f(x+\pi)$. Then $g$ is continuous as a
difference of continuous functions (the second is $f$ precomposed with the
continuous shift $x\mapsto x+\pi$). Using $2\pi$-periodicity,
\[
  g(x+\pi)=f(x+\pi)-f(x+2\pi)=f(x+\pi)-f(x)=-g(x),
\]
so $g$ is odd under the half-turn; in particular $g(0)=-g(\pi)$. Thus $g(0)$ and
$g(\pi)$ have opposite signs (or both vanish). If both vanish we are done at
$x=0$; otherwise $g$ takes a positive and a negative value on $[0,\pi]$, and by
the Intermediate Value Theorem there is $c\in[0,\pi]$ with $g(c)=0$, i.e.
$f(c)=f(c+\pi)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No strictly antipodal preference]
\label{thm:nostrict}
Let $f:\R\to\R$ be continuous and $2\pi$-periodic. Then it is not the case that
$f(x+\pi)<f(x)$ for all $x$; symmetrically, it is not the case that
$f(x)<f(x+\pi)$ for all $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $f(x+\pi)<f(x)$ for all $x$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:bu} there is $x_0$ with
$f(x_0)=f(x_0+\pi)$, contradicting the strict inequality at $x_0$. The symmetric
statement is identical with the inequality reversed.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:nostrict} is the correct and defensible remnant of the informal
``Borsuk--Ulam contradicts Pareto'' argument: on a circular (non-contractible)
opinion space, no continuous rule can strictly break every antipodal tie.

\section{Part II: disproof of ``continuous implies dictatorial''}
\label{sec:disproof}

All statements below concern the mean aggregator $\avg_n$.

\begin{lemma}[Unanimity/Pareto]\label{lem:unan}
For $n\ge 1$ and any constant $c$, $\avg_n(c,\dots,c)=c$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n c=\frac{nc}{n}=c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Anonymity]\label{lem:anon}
For any permutation $\sigma$ and any profile $p$,
$\avg_n(p\circ\sigma)=\avg_n(p)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A permutation reindexes the summands, and finite sums are invariant under
reindexing: $\sum_i p_{\sigma(i)}=\sum_i p_i$. Divide by $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Continuity]\label{lem:cont}
The map $\avg_n:\R^n\to\R$ is continuous.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is a finite sum of coordinate projections (each continuous) scaled by the
constant $1/n$; sums and scalar multiples of continuous functions are continuous.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity / strong Pareto]\label{lem:mono}
If $p_i\le q_i$ for all $i$, then $\avg_n(p)\le\avg_n(q)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Sums preserve the coordinatewise order, $\sum_i p_i\le\sum_i q_i$; dividing by the
positive constant $n$ preserves the inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Translation invariance]\label{lem:trans}
For $n\ge 1$, any profile $p$, and any constant $c$,
$\avg_n(p_1+c,\dots,p_n+c)=\avg_n(p)+c$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\frac{1}{n}\sum_i (p_i+c)=\frac{1}{n}\big(\sum_i p_i + nc\big)=\avg_n(p)+c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Non-dictatorship]\label{lem:nodict}
For $n\ge 2$ there is no agent $i$ with $\avg_n(p)=p_i$ for all profiles $p$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix any candidate dictator $i$. Consider $p$ with $p_i=0$ and $p_j=1$ for $j\ne
i$. Then $\avg_n(p)=\frac{0+(n-1)}{n}=\frac{n-1}{n}$. Since $n\ge 2$,
$0<\frac{n-1}{n}<1$, so $\avg_n(p)\ne 0=p_i$; hence $i$ is not a dictator. As $i$
was arbitrary, no dictator exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Continuous non-dictatorial aggregator exists]\label{thm:main}
For every $n\ge 2$ there exists an aggregation rule $F:\R^n\to\R$ that is
simultaneously continuous, unanimous (Pareto), anonymous, monotone,
translation-invariant, and non-dictatorial.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $F=\avg_n$ and combine Lemmas~\ref{lem:unan}--\ref{lem:nodict}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The conjecture ``any social choice function on $n$ alternatives is either
discontinuous or dictatorial'' is false.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For $n\ge 2$, Theorem~\ref{thm:main} provides a rule that is both continuous and
non-dictatorial.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion: where the topology actually lives}
\label{sec:discussion}

Two independent reasons explain why the literal slogan fails, and together they
locate the genuine mathematical content.

\subsection*{Domain topology is the seat of the obstruction}
Borsuk--Ulam produces a coincidence only on a non-contractible space---a sphere,
which cannot be continuously shrunk to a point. The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:bu}
depends essentially on periodicity, i.e. on the circle's topology. If the opinion
domain is contractible (a line, an interval, a simplex of von
Neumann--Morgenstern utilities, or any convex set), there is no antipodal
structure and no obstruction; Theorem~\ref{thm:main} exhibits a fully fair
continuous rule.

\subsection*{Discrete versus continuous}
Arrow's theorem concerns aggregation of discrete linear orders subject to
Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives. There is no ambient topology and no
continuity hypothesis in Arrow's setting, so a continuity-based argument cannot
literally be Arrow's theorem.

\subsection*{The correct topological theorems}
The genuine topological impossibility is Chichilnisky's theorem: there is no
continuous, anonymous, unanimity-respecting aggregation map $(S^1)^k\to S^1$ (more
generally $(S^n)^k\to S^n$). Later, Baryshnikov (1993) gave a genuinely
topological derivation of Arrow's discrete theorem by constructing a nerve/CW
model of the preference data and invoking obstructions of Borsuk--Ulam type. Thus
the honest form of the thesis is: topological obstructions of Borsuk--Ulam type
govern aggregation on spheres, and Arrow's discrete theorem can be recovered
through a geometric encoding of ranked preferences---but Arrow's theorem is not a
one-line corollary of Borsuk--Ulam, and continuity by itself never implies
dictatorship.

\section{Future directions}
Formalize Chichilnisky's theorem for $(S^1)^k\to S^1$ and connect it to the
one-dimensional kernel proved here; develop the nerve/CW model behind
Baryshnikov's derivation to make the discrete-to-topological bridge explicit; and
classify which opinion domains admit continuous fair aggregation as a function of
their homotopy type.

\section{Conclusion}
The slogan ``Arrow's impossibility is Borsuk--Ulam'' is, taken literally,
incorrect, and its derived claim ``continuous $\Rightarrow$ dictatorial'' is
false---the mean is a standing counterexample for all $n\ge 2$. Yet the
topological viewpoint is vindicated in its honest form: the one-dimensional
Borsuk--Ulam theorem genuinely forbids strictly antipodal continuous preferences
on a circle, and richer topological obstructions govern aggregation on spheres
and even reach back to Arrow's discrete theorem. Social choice really is
topology---provided one remembers that the decisive variable is the shape of the
space of opinions, not the ballot.

\end{document}

